    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d39">

                <pb facs="0089" n="69" xml:id="pg69"/>

                <head>39. Memorandum From the Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defense for
                    International Security Affairs (Bundy) to the Deputy Secretary of Defense
                        (Gilpatric)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d39-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">I-25146/62</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1962-01-31"><hi rendition="#italic">January 31,
                        1962</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Air Operations in South Vietnam</item>
                </list>
                <p>You gave me yesterday, for comment, General Taylorʼs request for a report on the
                    “Binh Hoa” operation of 21 January.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d39-f2"/> Action on
                    this request remains with General Lansdaleʼs office.</p>
                <p>Our inquiries from General Taylorʼs staff indicate that his concern stems from an
                    account brought back by Roger Hilsman to the effect that the operation was far
                    too much of a “set piece” and was not an effective form of counter-guerrilla
                        warfare.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d39-f3"/></p>
                <p>I see that Hilsman is appearing before the Special Group tomorrow,<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d39-f4"/> and urge that you bring out his criticisms to the fullest
                    extent. If necessary, I could arrange to follow up further.</p>
                <p>Depending on the validity of Hilsmanʼs criticisms, I think we should reconsider
                    the question of a guidance message to the air people in South Vietnam,
                    responding to Alexis Johnsonʼs letter mildly rebuking us for the Cambodian
                    village incident.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d39-f5"/> This came up at the SecDef/JCS
                    meeting on Monday, and you will recall the sentiment was against sending such a
                    message. However, my staff got the draft that had been prepared within the Joint
                    Staff, which I attach.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d39-f6"/> It seems to me a quite
                    reasonable message and not to interfere with the necessary freedom of
                    action.</p>
                <p>After the meeting tomorrow, I suggest that you reconsider whether such a message
                    would not be a good thing to do. A marginal benefit, of course, would be in our
                    relations with State.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">William P. Bundy</hi><anchor n="7"
                            xml:id="d39-f7"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d39-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d39-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Washington National Records Center, RG 330, OSD Files: FRC 71 A 6489
                    Sensitive 7-61. Top Secret. Secretary McNamara also received a copy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f2" n="2" xml:id="d39-f2-content">
                <p>Taylorʼs request, dated January 30, stated that the President wanted to learn
                    more about the Binh Hoa operation. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f3" n="3" xml:id="d39-f3-content">
                <p>For Hilsmanʼs report on the Binh Hoa operation, see <ref target="#d42">Document
                        42</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f4" n="4" xml:id="d39-f4-content">
                <p>The minutes of the meeting of the Special Group (CI) on February 1 indicate only
                    that Hilsman briefed the group on his recent trip to Vietnam and outlined his
                    February 2 paper. (Department of State, Special Group Counterinsurgency Files:
                    Lot 68 D 451, 1/1/62-7/31/62)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f5" n="5" xml:id="d39-f5-content">
                <p><ref target="#d32">Document 32</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f6" n="6" xml:id="d39-f6-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d39-f7" n="7" xml:id="d39-f7-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this stamped signature.</p>
            </note>
